# Agent Office — API credentials and provider settings.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in what you have. `.env` is gitignored; this file is
# the committed template and must never contain a real key.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# A real environment variable always beats a value in this file, so exporting a
# key for one command still works. Nothing here is required: the default
# provider is the local Claude Code CLI, which uses your existing sign-in
# (`claude auth login`) rather than a key.

# --- Anthropic ---------------------------------------------------------------
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com

# --- OpenAI ------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com

# --- DeepSeek ----------------------------------------------------------------
# DeepSeek serves the OpenAI Chat Completions shape, so it runs through the same
# adapter with a different endpoint and key.
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-...
# DEEPSEEK_MODEL=deepseek-chat
# DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com

# --- Shared ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cap on a single model response, applied to every HTTP provider.
# AGENT_OFFICE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192

# --- Access -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The server requires a token on every /api request and the WebSocket upgrade,
# because loopback is not a user boundary: another local account or process can
# open a socket to the port, and would otherwise be able to start billed runs and
# read every report.
#
# Leave this unset for normal use. `npm run serve` then generates a fresh token per
# process and prints it as a ready-to-open URL; `npm run web` picks it up through
# the Vite proxy with nothing to paste. Pin it only for CI and scripts that cannot
# read a token off stdout — a value chosen once outlives the process it protects.
# AGENT_OFFICE_TOKEN=

# --- Capabilities -------------------------------------------------------------
# Both of these are environment variables and NOT run options, deliberately:
# anything that can reach the local port can already start a run, so a per-run
# grant would let any local process hand itself the capability.

# Allow agents to run shell commands. Off by default: 114 of the 156 catalog agents
# request `Bash`, the CLI grants it as a real shell without pausing for approval,
# and a shell's first command can be `cd`. Accepts `1` or `true`.
# AGENT_OFFICE_ALLOW_SHELL=1

# Which MCP servers delegated agents may reach. Points at a file in the Claude Code
# format — one file serves both, so there is no second dialect to keep in sync:
#
#   { "mcpServers": { "investing": { "command": "python", "args": ["-m", "app.mcp.server"] } } }
#
# An agent may then be granted `mcp__investing__<tool>`; a request naming an
# unregistered server is refused by name. Agents never inherit the servers in your
# own ~/.claude.json or a project .mcp.json. Set-but-unreadable fails at startup
# rather than degrading to "no servers", because the silent version presents as
# "my agent ignores its tools" and sends you looking in the wrong place.
# AGENT_OFFICE_MCP_CONFIG=./mcp-servers.json

# --- Resolution overrides -----------------------------------------------------
# Pin the Claude Code binary. Resolution normally tries PATH, then the newest
# versioned install under %APPDATA% and the MSIX package path — see SETUP.md.
# AGENT_OFFICE_CLAUDE_BIN=

# Pin the Chrome/Edge binary used to print the PDF. Same idea: found automatically,
# overridable when the automatic answer is wrong.
# AGENT_OFFICE_CHROME_BIN=

# Treat another directory as the repository root — where `categories/`, `runs/` and
# `.env` are looked for. Used by the CLI scripts and the catalog manifest check.
# AGENT_OFFICE_REPO_ROOT=
